  "solution": "def forming_magic_square(s: list[list[int]]) -> int:\n    all_magic = [\n        [[8, 1, 6], [3, 5, 7], [4, 9, 2]],\n        [[6, 1, 8], [7, 5, 3], [2, 9, 4]],\n        [[4, 9, 2], [3, 5, 7], [8, 1, 6]],\n        [[2, 9, 4], [7, 5, 3], [6, 1, 8]],\n        [[8, 3, 4], [1, 5, 9], [6, 7, 2]],\n        [[4, 3, 8], [9, 5, 1], [2, 7, 6]],\n        [[6, 7, 2], [1, 5, 9], [8, 3, 4]],\n        [[2, 7, 6], [9, 5, 1], [4, 3, 8]]\n    ]\n    min_cost = float('inf')\n    for m in all_magic:\n        cost = sum(abs(s[i][j] - m[i][j]) for i in range(3) for j in range(3))\n        min_cost = min(min_cost, cost)\n    return min_cost\n",
  "explanation": "There are exactly 8 possible 3x3 magic squares using digits 1-9. Testing against all 8 yields minimal transition cost."
}